A||||10||Homicidal||Converting Altec Lansing PC Sub -----> Mobile Audio sub??||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||06-22-2000||04:29 PM||||Do you think I could do this???  It need an external power source (self amplified) and only has an RCA "signal in" jack.......<P>I know I'm nuts, but this thing sounds SOOOOOO good, throws better than most regular car subs.....and it's small, fits anywhere.......||||reg||1
Z||000001||Homicidal||06-22-2000||04:38 PM||||Hmmmm......on my amp there is a SUB-OUT connection.......I'm thinking I could use that for the lead in connection for the music.<P>Power is the only complicated factor since the sub has a wall plug.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Majik||06-22-2000||04:45 PM||||Most of the subs for computers are powered by around 9 volts...so head to radio shack and pick up the appropriate cigerette lighter type power adapter, and let loose.<P>I'm sure you could also hard wire this adapter into a "ignition" power source, so it isn't on all of the time, nor is it taking up your cigerette lighter.   Good idea...tell me how it works!<P>Majik||||reg||1
Z||000003||gkozlov||06-22-2000||04:56 PM||||I did this and It works.||||reg||1
Z||000004||gkozlov||06-22-2000||05:01 PM||||I unscrewed the back of the sub encloser and bypassed the power transformer. My built in Altec Lansing sub amp was designed  to run off 13v dc any way so I am running it straight of the battery. I have a toggle switch to control it. It fits nicely behind my passanger side seat and sounds great. The best part is it was so cheap to do.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||06-22-2000||05:17 PM||||This sounds like the best fiero idea in awhile,just make sure the power supply isn't AC,where the car is DC,but if one brand will work,I'll bet most others will also.<BR>Thanks for the tip.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||06-22-2000||09:46 PM||||I've thought about doing this, but like many things it's on hold right now.<P>Actually the lighter adaptor idea sounds good but isn't. those things usually can't handle the load decent PC speakers draw. My little Compaq ones for example use an amp and a half of current, peak is even higher. Sub woofers use more.<P>If your PC sub uses 12-13VDC internally and you bypass the AC supply that is built into many units, make sure you cut the lead from the AC supply. Don't just feed into it after the transformer or where ever. Also make sure it's on some fused circuit or better yet add a fuse inside the sub.<P>If the sub is transformer powered the best place to cut the AC out is after the rectifiers and before the filter capacitors. Feed the car DC to the filters. get the car DC from a switched circuit then you don't have to worry about leaving it on. (You might still want a control switch for it.)<P>If the PC sub uses a "switching" power supply then you have to be careful where you tap in. In that case you'll likely want to eliminate the whole switcher and add a filter cap if need be to the new 12 volt feed.<P>You could also paint these to match and just leave them in the front of the passengers foot well. some of these might even fit where the factory sub amp goes. would bulg the carpet a bit but should fit. that area of the tunnel is already depressed in some.<P>you could even extend the wires for the volume control on most PC subs and put the control where it's convienient. Then you'd be able to adjust it how you want it easy.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Homicidal||06-23-2000||12:34 AM||||One question:  how do I get it to receive the music "signals"?<P>Does anybody have or want to write a smal tutorial on how to do this?  <P>I think I can get the power supply part down, but the lead in for the music signal sounds hard since all it has is a "signal in" rca jack, the size of a pair of headphone for a Walkman.  <P>The wall plug is AC 120V and there is also a hole that is DC OUT 13V 2A.<P>What does this mean?||||reg||1
Z||000008||gkozlov||06-23-2000||01:06 AM||||Ok, here goes. Bypass the power transformer by feeding power directly to the amp from your vehicle regardless of where you draw power from. Thats up to you.<P>Second, go to toyr local Radio Shack and buy a Y plug (RCA's feeding to a stereo jack) that will be your sound source. If your using a decent aftermarket head unit, you will have at least one set of RCA outs. <P>When I hooked up my Altec Lansing sub, I had to get a noise filter. I had way too much noise. They hook right up to your RCA's. I also had to go to a 2 amp slow burn fuse. They also sell these at Radio Shack. I blew a fuse every time I cranked. Now I have no problems. <P>Mine hits real hard for a 6.5 in sub. I absolutely love it. I think I will add another under my dash, and maybe one behind the drivers seat. These subs can usually be baught of Ebay for about 20.00.<BR>If you need more help. Let me know and Ill try toget part numbers for the stuff I used.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Homicidal||06-23-2000||01:16 AM||||The only model # I see that could be useful is the ACS160.<P>The thing is, I already got a 10" sub and an amp in my car.  Can I feed the power directly off the amplifier?  Also, how do I input the music signal to the sub and bypass the amp?<P>Or do I use the "SUB OUT" RCA plug on my amp?||||reg||1
Z||000010||gkozlov||06-23-2000||09:43 AM||||The sub in the Altec Lansing is pretty small. I dont know exactly how much power it can handle so be careful when hooking up to your amp.<P>Since you already have a sub out on your amp use it instead of the out on your stereo. That way you still use the Altec Lansing amp. <BR>||||reg||1
